'''John Ernst Steinbeck'''<!-- <ref>{{cite book|last=French|first=Warren G. |title=John Steinbeck |publisher=Twayne Publishers|year=1975|page=20|isbn=0-8057-0693-3}}</ref><ref>{{cite book|last=St. Pierre|first=Brian |title=John Steinbeck, the California years |url=https://archive.org/details/johnsteinbeckcal00stpi|url-access=registration|publisher=Chronicle Books|year=1983|page=[https://archive.org/details/johnsteinbeckcal00stpi/page/11 11]|isbn=0-87701-281-4}}</ref> --> ({{IPAc-en|ˈ|s|t|aɪ|n|b|ɛ|k}} {{respell|STYNE|bek}}; February 27, 1902&nbsp;– December 20, 1968) was an American writer and novelist. He won the [[1962 Nobel Prize in Literature]] "for his realistic and imaginative writings, combining as they do sympathetic humor and keen social perception".<ref name="Literature1962">{{cite web|url=http://nobelprize.org/nobel_prizes/literature/laureates/1962/index.html|title=Nobel Prize in Literature 1962|publisher=Nobel Foundation|access-date=October 17, 2008|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081021034222/http://nobelprize.org/nobel_prizes/literature/laureates/1962/index.html|archive-date=October 21, 2008}}</ref> He has been called "a giant of American letters".<ref>{{cite news |title=Swedish Academy reopens controversy surrounding Steinbeck's Nobel prize |url=https://www.theguardian.com/books/2013/jan/03/swedish-academy-controversy-steinbeck-nobel |newspaper=[[The Guardian]] |date=January 3, 2013 |access-date=January 12, 2019 |archive-date=October 22, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131022042357/https://www.theguardian.com/books/2013/jan/03/swedish-academy-controversy-steinbeck-nobel |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="Why do children study Of Mice and Men?">{{cite news|title=Who, what, why: Why do children study Of Mice and Men?|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/magazine-12829392|publisher=BBC|access-date=December 6, 2014|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150107080104/http://www.bbc.com/news/magazine-12829392|archive-date=January 7, 2015|work=BBC News|date=March 25, 2011}}</ref> One of the most widely read authors of the 20th century, he was also an independent journalist and war correspondent during [[World War II]] and the [[Vietnam War]].

During his writing career, he authored 33 books, with one book coauthored alongside [[Edward Ricketts]], including 16 novels, 6 [[non-fiction book]]s, and 2 [[collections of short stories]]. He is widely known for the comic novels ''[[Tortilla Flat]]'' (1935) and ''[[Cannery Row (novel)|Cannery Row]]'' (1945), the epic ''[[East of Eden (novel)|East of Eden]]'' (1952), and the novellas ''[[The Red Pony]]'' (1933) and ''[[Of Mice and Men]]'' (1937). The [[Pulitzer Prize]]–winning ''[[The Grapes of Wrath]]'' (1939)<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.pulitzer.org/bycat/Novel|title=Novel|publisher=The Pulitzer Prizes|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080821041438/http://www.pulitzer.org/bycat/Novel|archive-date=August 21, 2008}}</ref> is considered Steinbeck's [[masterpiece]] and part of the [[Western canon|American literary canon]].<ref name="Sixteen Modern American Authors">{{cite book|last1=Bryer|first1=R. Jackson|title=Sixteen Modern American Authors, Volume 2|url=https://archive.org/details/sixteenmodername02tici|url-access=registration|date=1989|publisher=Duke University Press|location=Durham, NC|isbn=978-0-8223-1018-1|page=[https://archive.org/details/sixteenmodername02tici/page/620 620]}}</ref> By the 75th anniversary of its publishing date, it had sold 14 million copies.<ref name="Grapes of Wrath 10 facts">{{cite news|last1=Chilton|first1=Martin|title=The Grapes of Wrath: 10 surprising facts about John Steinbeck's novel|newspaper=The Telegraph|date=September 16, 2015|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/books/authors/john-steinbeck-grapes-of-wrath-what-you-should-know/|publisher=Telegraph (London)|access-date=October 13, 2022|archive-date=October 10, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221010203928/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/books/authors/john-steinbeck-grapes-of-wrath-what-you-should-know/|url-status=live}}</ref>

Much of Steinbeck's work employs [[Setting (narrative)|setting]]s in his native [[central California]], particularly in the [[Salinas Valley]] and the [[California Coast Ranges]] region. His works frequently explored the [[Theme (narrative)|themes]] of fate and injustice, especially as applied to downtrodden or [[everyman]] [[protagonist]]s. He was awarded with the [[Presidential Medal of Freedom]] from [[Lyndon B. Johnson]] in 1964 and was nominated for three [[Academy Award for Best Original Screenplay|Academy Awards]].

