'''Jerk''' (also known as '''jolt'''<ref>{{cite web |last=Jackson |first=George |title=What is a jolt in physics? |url=https://physics-network.org/what-is-a-jolt-in-physics/ |website=Physics Network |date=April 18, 2025 |access-date=May 11, 2025}}</ref>) is the rate of change of an object's [[acceleration]] over time. It is a [[vector quantity]] (having both magnitude and direction). Jerk is most commonly denoted by the symbol {{mvar|j}} and expressed in m/s<sup>3</sup> ([[SI unit]]s) or [[standard gravity|standard gravities]] per second<ref>{{cite web |title=What is the term used for the third derivative of position? |url=https://www.desy.de/user/projects/Physics/General/jerk.html |website=Usenet Physics FAQ |access-date=May 11, 2025}}</ref>(''g''<sub>0</sub>/s).

