[[File:97.- CCCLXXXII-1.1.7.0001 - Programa Ilustrado de los festejos oficiales y particulares.tif|thumb|upright=.7|A program of centennial festivities of Mexican independence in 1910, asserting the historical continuity of [[Miguel Hidalgo]], [[Benito Juárez]] "Law," and [[Porfirio Díaz]], "Peace"]]
The '''history of [[Mexico]]''' encompasses over 13,000 years between the initial migration of indigenous peoples and the modern day. Central and southern Mexico (termed [[Mesoamerica]]) saw the rise and fall of complex [[Indigenous peoples of Mexico|indigenous]] [[Civilization|civilizations]] such as the Olmecs, Mayas and Aztecs.  [[Mesoamerican]] civilizations developed glyph based [[Mesoamerican writing systems|writing systems]], recording the political history of conquests and rulers. Mesoamerican history prior to European arrival is variously called the pre-hispanic era and the [[pre-Columbian era]]. In the 16th Century, the [[Spanish conquest of the Aztec Empire]] resulted in the creation of [[New Spain]] and the introduction of [[Christianity]]. Following [[Mexican War of Independence|Mexico's independence]] from Spain in 1821, the country faced numerous challenges including conflicts between 'strongmen' or [[Caudillo|caudillos]] the [[Mexican–American War]], and the [[Second French intervention in Mexico|French invasion]]. In the early 19th century, [[Mexican Revolution]] resulted in the rise of the [[Institutional Revolutionary Party]] (PRI) which has served as the pre-eminent political party in the country until the modern day. Experiments with [[nationalization]], reforms, and development projects resulted in the [[Mexican miracle]] during the 1940s to 1970s. Since then, Mexico has been defined by its incorporation into the [[North American Free Trade Agreement]] (NAFTA) in 1994, the [[Mexican peso crisis]], and the [[Mexican drug war]].

